From d9e85d65e6e375b3408ca0c97107ae68e97c7ca2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:47:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: a substitution anywhere inside
 arithmetic is a shell sink
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`output_reaches_shell` counted a `$(` as a sink only in command position
(after `^ ; & | ( { \n then do else`). Inside arithmetic bash evaluates the
text recursively and an array subscript in it runs command substitutions, so a
substitution's OUTPUT is shell code wherever it sits:

    a=(1); x=$(( 1 + $(echo 'a[$(echo RAN >&2)]') ))    # prints RAN

On main `x=$(( 1 + $(echo 'a[$(bash BIG)]') ))` was sink=False and
scan_gateway_lifecycle allowed it with BIG over the scan cap (t_01a1d6ae05c19594).
PR #2626's `$(date +%s)` exemption also opened the date-prefixed shapes
`x=$(( $(date +%s) + $(echo 'a[$(bash BIG)]') ))` and
`(( $(date +%s) > $(echo …) ))` (review 5898137039), which main had refused
only through the accidental `(` anchor.

Fix: tools/shell_arith_sink.py, a small linear tokenizer (quotes, comments,
nested `$(`/`(`/`${` frames) that reports a `$(` whose nearest enclosing
context is arithmetic: `$(( ))`, `(( ))`/`for ((`, `$[ ]`, `let`,
`[[ … -eq|-ne|-lt|-le|-gt|-ge … ]]`, an array subscript (`a[…]=`,
`${a[…]}`), a `${x:offset:length}` substring, and (coarsely) any
`declare/local/typeset/readonly/export -i`. `$(date +%s)` stays exempt unless
the command can rebind `date`. `[ … -eq … ]`/`test` are not arithmetic (bash
5.2 reports "integer expression expected") and stay out, so the t_01a1d67ff0075752
watcher (`deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + 2700 ))`, `[ "$(date +%s)" -lt … ]`, big-log
greps) still passes. Anything the scanner cannot match only widens the
arithmetic region, so a miss fails closed.

Every new sink shape was checked in real bash 5.2.21 (RAN printed).
Out of scope, unchanged: a substitution stored in a variable and evaluated
later (`v=$(…); (( v ))`); the guard does not track data flow through
variables (`v=$(…); $v` is the same class).

Tests: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_arith_sink.py gains 22 must-sink shapes
(each also refused end to end with BIG over the cap), 16 must-not-sink shapes
and a mixed big-log case. RED on origin/main 2c34b9880a: 23 failed, 80 passed.
GREEN with the fix: 103 passed.
